<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jimmy Carter</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1924">1924</a>–<a href="/years/2024">2024</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jimmy Carter was the 39th president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, serving from 1977 to 1981. A Democrat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, he made human rights central to foreign policy, signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torrijos-carter-treaties">Torrijos–Carter Treaties</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> in 1977 and brokered the Camp <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Accords between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and Israel in 1978, but his presidency was weakened by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> hostage crisis. He won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 2002 for his later humanitarian work.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jimmy-carter.jpg" alt="Jimmy Carter" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Department of Defense. Department of the Navy. Naval Photographic Center · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jimmy_Carter_Official_Portrait2_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>James Earl Carter Jr. was born on 1 October 1924 in Plains, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, at the Wise Sanitarium, where his mother worked as a registered nurse. He was the eldest child of Bessie Lillian Gordy and James Earl Carter Sr., a local businessman who ran a general store. The family settled in nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">Archery</a>, a community populated almost entirely by impoverished Black families. His father was staunchly pro-segregation, yet allowed his son to befriend the children of the Black farmhands.</p><p>Carter graduated from the U.S. Naval Academy in 1946 and joined the submarine service. In 1952 he began an association with the Navy's nuclear submarine program, led by Captain Hyman G. Rickover, who he later said had the most influence on his life after his parents. He then returned to Plains and expanded the family peanut-growing business.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Carter won a seat in the Georgia State Senate in 1962, after a repeat election because early counting was shown to be the result of fraudulent voting. He served from 1963 to 1967. Sworn in as the 76th governor of Georgia on 12 January 1971, he declared in his inaugural speech that the time for racial discrimination was over, which caused many segregationists who had supported him to feel betrayed. He merged about 300 state agencies into 22.</p><p>He announced his presidential campaign on 12 December 1974. Little known outside his home state, he was derided with the question Jimmy Who?. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/watergate-scandal">Watergate scandal</a> fresh in voters' minds, his position as an outsider helped him, and he narrowly defeated the Republican incumbent Gerald Ford.</p><p>Inaugurated on 20 January 1977, Carter issued an unconditional amnesty for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a> draft evaders. His administration negotiated the Camp David Accords, the Panama Canal Treaties and the second Strategic Arms Limitation Talks agreement, established diplomatic relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Departments</a> of Energy and Education. His term was marked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> and recession, the Soviet invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> hostage crisis. He lost the 1980 election to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronald-reagan">Ronald Reagan</a> in a landslide.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the presidency Carter established the Carter Center to promote human rights, and he received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 2002. He travelled widely to conduct peace negotiations, monitor elections and combat neglected tropical diseases, and he was a key figure in Habitat for Humanity. He died at his home in Plains on 29 December 2024, aged 100, and was buried next to his wife Rosalynn. Historians have generally rated his presidency below average, while his post-presidency, the longest in U.S. history, is viewed more favourably.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jimmy Carter” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the late 1970s military regimes gave way to civilian government, encouraged by pressure from the United States under President <b>Jimmy Carter</b> and, after 1989, by the end of the Cold War.</blockquote><a href="/government/military-junta">Military junta · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Andy Williams recorded it at Robert Kennedy's funeral in 1968, and it was sung at the burial of Lyndon B. Johnson in 1973 and at <b>Jimmy Carter</b>'s funeral ceremony in 2025.</blockquote><a href="/songs/battle-hymn-of-the-republic">Battle Hymn of the Republic</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Jimmy Carter</b> said that in many countries he heard it used almost as often as national anthems; Garth Brooks and Trisha Yearwood sang it at his state funeral in January 2025.</blockquote><a href="/songs/imagine">Imagine</a></li><li><blockquote>The crisis had overshadowed <b>Jimmy Carter</b>'s final year in office.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-20">20 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Fifty-two were held for 444 days, a crisis that soured US-Iranian relations and damaged <b>Jimmy Carter</b>'s presidency.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-4">4 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It has trained American naval officers ever since; <b>Jimmy Carter</b> was among its graduates.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-10">10 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lillian G. Carter Nursing Center, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Jimmy Carter House, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.04,-84.39&amp;year=1989" aria-label="Lillian G. Carter Nursing Center on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2378 998 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jimmy Carter"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2828 1268h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2828 1268h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.04,-84.39&amp;year=1989">What was here in 1989? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
